and wittiness for standing
up against the establishment.

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:57.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not like stand up
against the establishment

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.952 align:start size:97% position:3%
just to do it, but like
corruption and establishment

00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:02.221 align:start size:97% position:3%
taking advantage of
people, that kind of thing.

00:12:02.221 --> 00:12:05.458 align:start size:88% position:13%
But now you see these
guys, they're like, "Oh,

00:12:05.458 --> 00:12:08.627
dude these checks
are pretty large.

00:12:08.627 --> 00:12:12.698
Maybe we'll become
the establishment."

00:12:12.698 --> 00:12:17.703
It's just a weird mutation
to see in front of our eyes.

00:12:18.804 --> 00:12:20.272 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And you see it
with so many of them.

00:12:20.272 --> 00:12:21.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, Sarah Silverman's
another one, like these people

00:12:21.474 --> 00:12:23.242 align:start size:88% position:13%
who were, they were
edgy, like they went for

00:12:23.242 --> 00:12:25.544
it's not just like, "Oh,
I was kind of like out

00:12:25.544 --> 00:12:28.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
there a little," I'm talking
like the fringe case edgy

00:12:28.114 --> 00:12:30.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
comedians who then became
the most politically correct.

00:12:30.883 --> 00:12:32.284
It's, I could do
all day on that.

00:12:32.284 --> 00:12:34.954
Do you ever long for
the old days of comedy?

00:12:34.954 --> 00:12:39.391
Like if you were to open
up my YouTube on my TV,

00:12:39.391 --> 00:12:41.427
I'm watching old Johnny Carson.

00:12:41.427 --> 00:12:44.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm watching all the legends
of years long gone by.

00:12:44.864 --> 00:12:48.067
But the idea that you could do
stand up for a couple years,

00:12:48.067 --> 00:12:50.836
you get that five minutes,
you sit down with Carson,

00:12:50.836 --> 00:12:52.304
you get the sick.

00:12:52.304 --> 00:12:54.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was something really
nice to the machine.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:58.344 align:start position:0%
As beautiful as all this freedom
is, where in our day it's

00:12:59.378 --> 00:13:00.813
like there is nobody to go to.

00:13:00.813 --> 00:13:03.082 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's nobody's that I
don't care to be on Kimmel or

00:13:03.082 --> 00:13:04.683
Colbert or anything else,

00:13:04.683 --> 00:13:06.152
which is exactly
what you're saying.

00:13:06.152 --> 00:13:07.353
- Yeah.

00:13:07.353 --> 00:13:08.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But there was a
magic to it in a way.

00:13:08.788 --> 00:13:13.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. There's the nostalgia
that was the olden days

00:13:14.727 --> 00:13:16.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was never a professional
comedian during that time.

00:13:16.762 --> 00:13:19.431 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it's just all
looking back and I think

00:13:19.431 --> 00:13:22.201
it's a cool time
to reminisce about,

00:13:22.201 --> 00:13:27.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there's no part of me
that wishes it was that day,

00:13:27.173 --> 00:13:28.974
outside of like
the cool moments.

00:13:28.974 --> 00:13:32.545
Like if you got to sit down
with Carson, like, dude,

00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:35.014
of course, like that's amazing.

00:13:35.014 --> 00:13:38.818
But there was so much
outsourced power then,

00:13:38.818 --> 00:13:43.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
people couldn't take control
of their own destiny.

00:13:43.189 --> 00:13:45.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think self
responsibility is the

00:13:45.357 --> 00:13:47.293
greatest superpower people have.

00:13:47.293 --> 00:13:51.030 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it's the
most underutilized
power in our country.

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:54.033
People are waking
up to it, luckily.

00:13:54.033 --> 00:13:56.435
But I love the idea of like,

00:13:56.435 --> 00:13:59.572
now you take destiny
in your own hands.

00:13:59.572 --> 00:14:01.307
If you work hard,
you're consistent,

00:14:01.307 --> 00:14:04.343
you put stuff out online.

00:14:04.343 --> 00:14:09.148
If you're consistent with it
and you have some level of DNA

00:14:09.148 --> 00:14:14.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
to actually do comedy, you
will build an audience.

00:14:15.354 --> 00:14:18.224
You will, whether it's
big, small, fast, low,

00:14:18.224 --> 00:14:23.028
that's a variable, but
nobody needs to wait

00:14:23.028 --> 00:14:27.166
around being discovered
by the industry anymore.

00:14:27.166 --> 00:14:31.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I just love the empowerment
aspect of how it is today

00:14:31.971 --> 00:14:35.975
that censorships dragon in that.

00:14:35.975 --> 00:14:39.278
But for aside from that,
we have a lot of power

00:14:39.278 --> 00:14:40.980
in our hands and I love it.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:42.648
- Yeah. And it's on
you and it's like

00:14:42.648 --> 00:14:44.917
if you work harder and you
get better at your craft

00:14:44.917 --> 00:14:46.418 align:start size:84% position:16%
some good things
will probably happen.

00:14:46.418 --> 00:14:50.422
Not always, but I'd rather
it be on me than on an agent

00:14:50.422 --> 00:14:53.259 align:start size:84% position:16%
or on a machinery
that I can't control.

00:14:53.259 --> 00:14:55.294
Let me, let's jump off
comedy for just a sec

00:14:55.294 --> 00:14:57.129
cuz you are in Texas.

00:14:57.129 --> 00:14:58.631
- Yeah.

00:14:58.631 --> 00:15:02.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
- But you are in the Austin
area of Texas, the weird area.

00:15:02.368 --> 00:15:03.569
- Yeah.

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:05.070 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And when I talk to
other people from Texas,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:07.606
let's say my friends at
the Blaze over in Dallas,

00:15:07.606 --> 00:15:10.175 align:start position:0%
they often wonder what the
high hell's going on in Austin.

00:15:10.175 --> 00:15:13.846
So what is it like to be in
a sort of blueish area in a

00:15:13.846 --> 00:15:15.981
state that hopefully is
about to go even redder?

00:15:15.981 --> 00:15:17.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
We'll see what happens
over the next couple days.

00:15:17.983 --> 00:15:20.619
- Yeah. But we'll redder
the better in my opinion.

00:15:20.619 --> 00:15:23.455
And that's not just my ginger
lights politics talking Dave.

00:15:23.455 --> 00:15:24.924
(Dave laughing)

00:15:24.924 --> 00:15:28.160
- By the way, the hair matching
the flag behind you, really.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:31.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Thank you. I'm grateful,
I was born in America,

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:36.168
sucked to be born in Ukraine
with incompatible flag colors.

00:15:37.603 --> 00:15:41.774
I love Austin. Austin has
some of the best people

00:15:41.774 --> 00:15:46.779 align:start position:0%
who are purpose driven,
soulful, and really going after

00:15:48.047 --> 00:15:51.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
their dreams and contributing
to a better world.

00:15:51.884 --> 00:15:56.889 align:start position:0%
Now there's certainly some
leftist stuff about how the city

00:15:58.057 --> 00:16:00.893
of Austin is run that
I don't agree with.

00:16:00.893 --> 00:16:05.864
And you certainly get some,
plenty of eyes glazed over

00:16:07.032 --> 00:16:10.703
mass formation, hip
psychosis addicts.

00:16:10.703 --> 00:16:13.973 align:start size:97% position:3%
But you know what I actually
kinda like that Dave,

00:16:13.973 --> 00:16:17.242
because nobody, I think
maybe we all wanna live

00:16:17.242 --> 00:16:20.512
in an echo chamber, but one
of the worst things for us

00:16:20.512 --> 00:16:22.982
would be to just live
in an echo chamber.

00:16:22.982 --> 00:16:26.452 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I, true diversity
in my opinion,

00:16:26.452 --> 00:16:31.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
is having a diversity of
perspectives and I like that.

00:16:32.825 --> 00:16:35.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
But at the same time,
because Austin is in Texas,

00:16:35.694 --> 00:16:38.630
there's plenty of
like-minded people.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:40.566
I've talked to people
all over the country

00:16:40.566 --> 00:16:44.136
and one of the biggest
pains that folks report

00:16:45.037 --> 00:16:46.839
is I feel like I'm the only one

00:16:46.839 --> 00:16:50.109
someone living in San
Francisco, Portland,

00:16:50.109 --> 00:16:52.511
so there's a lot
of community here.

00:16:52.511 --> 00:16:55.681
And a few months ago
we moved to a ranch

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:58.183
about an hour outside of Austin.

00:16:58.183 --> 00:17:01.387
So now it's, I mean we're
in real Texas country now.

00:17:01.387 --> 00:17:02.621
(both laughing)

00:17:02.621 --> 00:17:06.458
So it's just, if
someone drives by,

00:17:06.458 --> 00:17:10.462 align:start size:94% position:6%
we were just in downtown,
a town where we live

00:17:10.462 --> 00:17:13.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a coffee shop and
someone drove by with a vote

00:17:13.565 --> 00:17:16.368
for Bedo bumper sticker.

00:17:16.368 --> 00:17:19.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's just so cool how
like, dude, that's shocking.

00:17:19.938 --> 00:17:22.074
We saw that out here.

00:17:22.074 --> 00:17:23.609
- Yeah.

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:25.611
- Someone driving by with
a big Trump bumper sticker

00:17:25.611 --> 00:17:27.846
in downtown Portland.

00:17:27.846 --> 00:17:31.550
- It's funny cuz I'm here in
Florida, which is obviously

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.186
it's DeSantis country,
DeSantis stand depending

00:17:34.186 --> 00:17:35.154
on who you talk to.

00:17:36.321 --> 00:17:37.556
And there's one guy
in my neighborhood

00:17:37.556 --> 00:17:39.591
with the Charlie Chris
sign, and it's like,

00:17:39.591 --> 00:17:41.693
it actually, of course it
kind of seems funny to me,

00:17:41.693 --> 00:17:43.996
but I keep thinking one day
I'm gonna be walking my dog

00:17:43.996 --> 00:17:47.800
and I wanna meet this person
because what could possibly

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.903
be going on in their head
to live in the freest place

00:17:50.903 --> 00:17:52.071
and not want that freedom.

00:17:52.071 --> 00:17:55.140 align:start size:88% position:13%
However, I respect
their desire, of course,

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.109
to vote however they wish.

00:17:57.109 --> 00:18:01.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. That I like to
understand what, like, cool,

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.249
why one of my best friends,

00:18:04.249 --> 00:18:08.120 align:start position:0%
we were sitting around election
night about two years ago

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:11.523
and we're just talking like,
"Hey, here's who I voted for."

00:18:11.523 --> 00:18:14.693
And just one person
outta the group said,

00:18:14.693 --> 00:18:16.462
"Yeah, I voted for Biden."

00:18:16.462 --> 00:18:20.699
And it was so cool to just want
to understand him and it was

00:18:20.699 --> 00:18:24.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
a beautiful connective
conversation and
we didn't agree,

00:18:24.837 --> 00:18:27.372 align:start size:84% position:16%
but that didn't mean
we had to divide

00:18:27.372 --> 00:18:28.874
or not like each other.

00:18:28.874 --> 00:18:33.145
It was very interesting
to learn, "Oh, that's why

00:18:33.145 --> 00:18:35.214
you voted for Biden", by
the way, talk to him now.

00:18:35.214 --> 00:18:37.983 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he is like,
"Dude, I regret it."

00:18:37.983 --> 00:18:40.319
(both laughing)

00:18:40.319 --> 00:18:43.055
- Him along with apparently
81 million other people.

00:18:43.055 --> 00:18:47.392
I think a lot of the, I don't
know if all 81, but you know.

00:18:48.827 --> 00:18:52.998
In terms of doing comedy for
let's say a more conservative

00:18:52.998 --> 00:18:54.800
or libertarian minded crowd,

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:57.269
do you find that there are
rails that you can't touch on

00:18:57.269 --> 00:18:59.805 align:start size:88% position:13%
that side that maybe
you wish you could touch

00:18:59.805 --> 00:19:01.373 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you can't quite
get to because

00:19:01.373 --> 00:19:03.509
of maybe more religious issues,

00:19:03.509 --> 00:19:05.010
that kind of thing?

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:09.815
- I'm sure there's rails,
but I haven't hit them yet.

00:19:12.384 --> 00:19:16.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
Just recently, I've
really noticed, especially

00:19:16.989 --> 00:19:20.559 align:start size:84% position:16%
in my standup, I'm
shifting the material,

00:19:20.559 --> 00:19:24.329
releases special and got now
generate a lot of new material

00:19:24.329 --> 00:19:27.599
and I'm shifting the
material from doing,

00:19:27.599 --> 00:19:31.170
sometimes bashing the
left as a butt of jokes

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:35.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
to now it's much more self
deprecating of a conservative,

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:39.011 align:start size:88% position:13%
libertarian minded
person, which is great.

00:19:39.011 --> 00:19:43.315
I mean, I love to honor what
I love by making fun of it.

00:19:43.315 --> 00:19:45.951
And also it's like, if you
can't laugh at yourself,

00:19:45.951 --> 00:19:47.586
you've got problems.

00:19:47.586 --> 00:19:52.357 align:start size:97% position:3%
So as I'm doing that, I
haven't found the rails yet

00:19:52.357 --> 00:19:55.994
though in the past I
can say when before

00:19:55.994 --> 00:19:58.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
I got into politics,
I'm doing standup

00:19:58.063 --> 00:20:03.068 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I would do some level
of Jesus kind of joke there.

00:20:04.436 --> 00:20:06.939 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can tell like, all right,
there's some folks who,

00:20:06.939 --> 00:20:10.943 align:start size:97% position:3%
that was a hot button for
them and maybe it came across

00:20:10.943 --> 00:20:13.478
disrespectfully, maybe
it was just a good joke

00:20:13.478 --> 00:20:14.680
and they didn't like it.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know. But
the past experiences,

00:20:17.716 --> 00:20:21.186
I think the religious
piece probably is the

00:20:21.186 --> 00:20:25.057 align:start size:84% position:16%
biggest challenge
when it comes to the

00:20:25.057 --> 00:20:29.661
conservative crowd
to, dance well with

00:20:29.661 --> 00:20:31.363
as opposed to just offend.

00:20:31.363 --> 00:20:34.166 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, it's super
interesting because obviously

00:20:34.166 --> 00:20:37.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
my credentials and biography
are not fully in line

00:20:37.869 --> 00:20:39.705 align:start size:84% position:16%
with say what a
conservative audience

00:20:39.705 --> 00:20:41.240
would be showing up to.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:43.342
And yet I get nothing but
love from these people.

00:20:43.342 --> 00:20:44.876
- Yeah.

00:20:44.876 --> 00:20:47.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And even when I get close
to whatever that line might be

00:20:47.079 --> 00:20:49.514 align:start position:0%
or say something that makes
them a little bit uncomfortable

00:20:49.514 --> 00:20:50.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they haven't
thought about before,

00:20:50.716 --> 00:20:52.584
it's like, it's all good.

00:20:52.584 --> 00:20:54.820
There really is a sense
of like, it's all good.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
Yeah, we get that, we can
all be a little bit different

00:20:57.389 --> 00:20:58.957
and it's okay.

00:20:58.957 --> 00:21:02.995
- Yeah. And I would guess
with people who watch you

00:21:02.995 --> 00:21:07.165
see you and same with
me, they're watching us

00:21:07.165 --> 00:21:08.734
because they already know us.

00:21:08.734 --> 00:21:11.770 align:start size:81% position:19%
They love us, they
appreciate us.

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:14.806
So there's already
a big rapport built

00:21:14.806 --> 00:21:18.043 align:start size:97% position:3%
out of the countless videos
of yours they've seen.

00:21:18.043 --> 00:21:22.948 align:start position:0%
So there's that trust as
opposed to just a comedian fresh

00:21:22.948 --> 00:21:25.050
off the street going
into the showroom,

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.986
the audience doesn't know
who they are, less trust.

00:21:27.986 --> 00:21:30.889
So they can probably
get away with less

00:21:30.889 --> 00:21:33.925 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they just don't
know exactly where

00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:35.661
that person's heart is.

00:21:35.661 --> 00:21:36.962 align:start size:84% position:16%
But they know where
your heart is,

00:21:36.962 --> 00:21:38.630
they know where my heart is.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:41.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How many years did you spend
grinding through the clubs?

00:21:41.033 --> 00:21:45.537
I did it for a long time,
man, I started in May of 98

00:21:45.537 --> 00:21:48.006
right after college, a week
after graduating college.

00:21:48.006 --> 00:21:51.243
And I was doing standup for
many, for at least a decade,

00:21:51.243 --> 00:21:52.711
like six nights a week.

00:21:52.711 --> 00:21:54.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then the last couple
years before I left New York,

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:56.248
then I was a little bit less,

00:21:56.248 --> 00:21:57.616
started getting into podcasting.

00:21:57.616 --> 00:22:00.185
I saw the frontier, the
new frontier developing

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:01.787
eventually moved out
to LA now back here.

00:22:01.787 --> 00:22:04.423
But how many years were
you like in the grind of,

00:22:04.423 --> 00:22:07.826
you're one of 10 on the show,
they don't know who you are

00:22:07.826 --> 00:22:09.261
by the time you get
going, you gotta

00:22:09.261 --> 00:22:11.029
get off stage, et cetera?

00:22:11.029 --> 00:22:12.364
- Zero.

00:22:12.364 --> 00:22:13.231
- Zero.

00:22:13.231 --> 00:22:14.066
(JP laughing)

00:22:14.066 --> 00:22:15.334
Wow, I admire that.

00:22:15.334 --> 00:22:18.904
- The route I took,
I so don't recommend,

00:22:18.904 --> 00:22:21.039
but here's the route I took.

00:22:21.039 --> 00:22:26.044
I got known on YouTube and
just online videos first.

00:22:27.679 --> 00:22:31.817
So the audience was built up
and then I got into standup

00:22:31.817 --> 00:22:35.987
and I had already sort of
been doing a bit of standup

00:22:35.987 --> 00:22:39.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
before I knew it because
once I started getting known,

00:22:39.257 --> 00:22:41.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
different conferences
would come in,

00:22:41.693 --> 00:22:43.729
hire me to come in and speak.

00:22:43.729 --> 00:22:45.664
And they're like, "Oh, why
don't you do some comedy?"

00:22:45.664 --> 00:22:47.833
I'm like, well, "Cool
that makes sense."

00:22:47.833 --> 00:22:50.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I was practicing it
for a while without having

00:22:50.969 --> 00:22:54.072
it in the frame of
my mind as standup.

00:22:54.072 --> 00:22:57.309
And so as I was
starting standup,

00:22:57.309 --> 00:23:01.713
I already had an audience and
here's what I don't recommend,

00:23:01.713 --> 00:23:04.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just jumped into the
deep end cuz I didn't know

00:23:04.649 --> 00:23:06.451
any better, it's
like, "all right,

00:23:06.451 --> 00:23:08.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's start
headlining shows now."

00:23:08.687 --> 00:23:09.955
(Dave laughing)

00:23:09.955 --> 00:23:12.391
And I'm doing five
shows in this city

00:23:12.391 --> 00:23:15.360
rather than just starting slow.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:18.697
So it was very stressful.

00:23:18.697 --> 00:23:22.367
